# Africa batch (1441-1904): every brief rewritten to rules zero to four.
OVERRIDE = True
A = {
"af14410": dict(
 bluf="Portuguese Prince Henry the Navigator sent captains down the West African coast for gold and a sea route round Muslim-held trade. In 1441 they seized captives on the coast of today's Mauritania, and within a few years Portugal was buying them from African rulers. The Atlantic slave trade that began there moved about 12.5 million Africans over four centuries, built the plantation economies of the Americas, enriched European merchants and states and drained much of Atlantic Africa.",
 now="The trade shaped the population of the Americas (Brazil has the largest population of African descent outside Africa), created the racial hierarchies slavery required, and underlies today's debates over reparations and Africa's long-run development.",
 story="Portugal's ships had been working down the West African coast for a decade in search of gold. In 1441 an expedition sent by Portuguese Prince Henry the Navigator seized Africans on the coast of today's Mauritania and sold them in Portugal. A public sale of captives followed at Lagos in Portugal in 1444. Within decades Portugal was buying captives from African states along the coast.",
 why="Portugal was a small, poor kingdom seeking gold and a sea route round Muslim-controlled trade. Slavery already existed in Africa, the Mediterranean and the Islamic world, so Portuguese captains could tap existing markets. Within a few years they were buying captives from African rulers instead of raiding.",
 trig="An expedition sent by Prince Henry seized Africans on the coast of today's Mauritania in 1441.",
 then="Portugal shipped enslaved Africans to the sugar islands of Madeira and São Tomé, where the plantation model of one export crop worked by enslaved labour took shape; Brazil and the Caribbean copied it. Demand for captives fed wars between African states, which sold prisoners for guns, cloth and metal.",
 fact="Royal chronicler Gomes Eanes de Zurara recorded the Lagos sale of 1444, 235 captives divided on the quayside, and wrote that the parting of families moved the crowd to tears."),
"af14641": dict(
 bluf="Songhai King Sonni Ali set out to take the Niger River cities from a fading Mali Empire and with them the trade in salt and gold. He took Timbuktu in 1468 and Djenné after a long siege, and by his death in 1492 he held the great bend of the Niger. Songhai became West Africa's largest empire, built on control of the river, the trans-Saharan routes and the cities that taxed them.",
 now="Songhai set the template for state power in the Sahel: control of the river, the salt and gold routes, and the cities that taxed them. The same corridors are now contested by jihadist groups, smugglers and three juntas.",
 story="The Mali Empire was losing its hold on the Niger bend to succession disputes and to Tuareg and Mossi raids. Songhai King Sonni Ali, on the throne from 1464, led cavalry and a war fleet on the river. He seized Timbuktu in 1468 and, after a long siege, Djenné, the trading and learning cities of Mali. By his death in 1492 Songhai controlled the great bend of the Niger.",
 why="Mali was weakened by succession disputes and by attacks from the Tuareg and the Mossi. Whoever held the Niger bend controlled the exchange of Saharan salt for gold from the forest zone to the south.",
 then="Songhai dominated the region for a century. A Moroccan army with firearms defeated it at Tondibi in 1591, and the region broke into smaller states.",
 fact="Tradition holds that Sonni Ali drowned crossing a flooded river in 1492. Mali and Niger both claim Songhai as national heritage."),
"af14822": dict(
 bluf="Portuguese King João II wanted the Akan forest's gold bought at the coast rather than carried north across the Sahara, and Castile kept away from it. His commander Diogo de Azambuja built São Jorge da Mina on the Gold Coast in 1482 with stone cut in Portugal. Elmina was the first permanent European fort south of the Sahara, and the chain of coastal forts that copied it became the channel through which Europeans bought gold and later shipped captives from West Africa.",
 now="Elmina set the pattern of the European presence in West Africa for three centuries: forts on the shore, trade with African states behind them and no rule inland. That pattern held until the conquests of the 1870s to 1900s.",
 story="Gold from the Akan forest had gone north across the Sahara for centuries. In 1482 Portuguese King João II sent commander Diogo de Azambuja with pre-cut stone to build São Jorge da Mina on the Gold Coast, after negotiating with a reluctant local Akan ruler. The fort gave Portugal a secure base to buy gold at the coast. Later it held enslaved Africans awaiting ships to the Americas.",
 why="Portugal wanted the gold diverted from the desert routes to the sea. It wanted rivals, Castile above all, kept away.",
 then="Gold flowed to Lisbon and financed Portugal's expansion. The Dutch captured Elmina in 1637 and expanded its role in the slave trade; dozens of European forts followed along the coast.",
 fact="Elmina is the oldest European building south of the Sahara. Captives passed through its 'Door of No Return', and it and Cape Coast Castle are central sites of memory for the African diaspora."),
"af14883": dict(
 bluf="Portuguese King João II sent navigator Bartolomeu Dias to find the southern end of Africa and a sea route to the spices of Asia. Storms carried Dias past the Cape in 1488, and his exhausted crew forced him back near today's Mossel Bay. The Atlantic and Indian Oceans were proven to connect, and the route that bypassed Mediterranean and Middle Eastern middlemen made Portugal an Indian Ocean power within twenty years.",
 now="The Cape route moved the spice trade from Venice and Cairo to Lisbon within two decades and made the Indian Ocean a theatre of European naval power until the Suez Canal opened in 1869. When Suez or the Red Sea is blocked, as during the Houthi attacks on shipping from 2023, ships return to it.",
 story="Portugal had spent decades working down the African coast. In 1488 navigator Bartolomeu Dias, sent by Portuguese King João II, was driven south by storms and found he had rounded the southern tip of Africa. He reached the coast near today's Mossel Bay. His exhausted crew forced him to turn back.",
 why="Portugal had spent decades working down the African coast, funded by gold and slaves, looking for a way into the spice trade.",
 then="Vasco da Gama used the route to reach India in 1498. Portugal was an Indian Ocean power within twenty years. The Cape later became a supply station for Dutch and British shipping and the base of white settlement in South Africa.",
 fact="Dias named it the Cape of Storms and King João renamed it the Cape of Good Hope. Dias drowned in a storm off the Cape in 1500."),
"af14934": dict(
 bluf="Songhai general Muhammad Ture overthrew Sonni Ali's son in 1493 with the backing of Muslim merchants and clerics and took the title Askia. He built a professional army, provincial governors and a centralised Islamic state, and Timbuktu under his rule drew learned men from across the Muslim world. Songhai reached its peak as a state, and West Africa its fullest integration into the wider Muslim world.",
 now="Askia's state fixed the alliance of Muslim rulers, merchants and jurists that governed the Sahel's cities until the Moroccan conquest of 1591. The Timbuktu manuscripts, on law, astronomy and medicine, are the record of it and refute the idea of a pre-colonial Africa without written history.",
 story="Songhai King Sonni Ali died in 1492 and his son succeeded him. General Muhammad Ture, with support from Muslim merchants and clerics who had been uneasy under Sonni Ali, defeated the son in 1493 and took the title Askia. Askia Muhammad I organised a professional army and a provincial administration and promoted Islamic learning. Timbuktu's mosques and libraries drew learned men from across the Muslim world.",
 why="Muslim merchants and clerics had been uneasy under Sonni Ali, who held to traditional religion alongside Islam, and backed a ruler of their own faith. A standing army and governors let Songhai tax trade across a wider empire than cavalry raids could hold.",
 trig="Askia defeated Sonni Ali's son, Sonni Baru, in 1493.",
 then="Askia made the pilgrimage to Mecca in 1496-97 and returned with recognition as caliph of the western Sudan. He expanded the empire. His sons deposed him in 1528, and succession struggles weakened Songhai, which fell to Morocco in 1591.",
 fact="Timbuktu produced hundreds of thousands of manuscripts. In 2012 librarians smuggled many out in rice sacks to save them from jihadists, who burned some of the rest in 2013."),
"af15055": dict(
 bluf="Portuguese viceroy Francisco de Almeida was sent to seize the gold of Sofala and bases on the route to India, and to force the Swahili cities to pay tribute. His fleet stormed and plundered Kilwa in 1505 and installed a ruler loyal to Portugal; Mombasa was sacked the same year. The city-states that had linked Africa to the Indian Ocean for centuries lost their independence, and the ocean's trade was redirected by force to Lisbon.",
 now="The sack of Kilwa began three centuries of European naval control of the Indian Ocean trade; the Swahili cities lost their independence and the ocean's commerce was redirected to Lisbon, then Amsterdam, then London.",
 story="Kilwa Kisiwani, an island city in today's Tanzania, had grown rich on gold from the Zimbabwe plateau shipped through Sofala and had minted its own coins. In 1505 the fleet of Portuguese viceroy Francisco de Almeida stormed and plundered it. Almeida installed a ruler loyal to Portugal. Mombasa was sacked the same year.",
 why="Portugal wanted the gold of Sofala and bases on the route to India. It tried to force the Muslim merchant cities to pay tribute and buy trade licences.",
 then="Kilwa declined. Portugal held Fort Jesus at Mombasa until Omani Arabs drove it out in 1698; Oman, and later Zanzibar, then dominated the coast.",
 fact="Kilwa's ruins are a World Heritage site; Swahili, with its Arabic and Persian loanwords, records the trade the city lived on."),
"af15267": dict(
 bluf="Kongo King Afonso I wrote to Portuguese King João III in 1526 to stop Portuguese traders from São Tomé buying and seizing his subjects, nobles among them. Lisbon ignored him, and Afonso settled for royal inspection of slave sales rather than ending them. The trade enriched provincial lords beyond the king's control, and Kongo, a Christian state that dealt with Lisbon and Rome as an equal, was weakened from within by a commerce it could not govern.",
 now="His letters are among the earliest African written protests against the slave trade. They complicate any story of Africans as either passive victims or willing partners.",
 story="Kongo King Afonso I, born Nzinga Mbemba, made Christianity Kongo's state religion and exchanged envoys with Lisbon and Rome. Portuguese traders based on São Tomé were buying and seizing his subjects, even nobles, and paying with goods that let provincial chiefs bypass the king. In 1526 he wrote to Portuguese King João III asking him to stop it.",
 why="The trade enriched provincial lords and Portuguese merchants beyond royal control. It drained the king's population and undercut his authority.",
 then="Afonso set up royal inspection of slave sales. Portugal ignored his pleas. Kongo was weakened by slaving and succession wars; Portugal founded Angola to the south in 1575 as a slaving base and defeated Kongo at Mbwila in 1665.",
 fact="Afonso's letter told João III that 'our country is being completely depopulated'. His son Henrique had been consecrated in Rome in 1518 as the first bishop from sub-Saharan Africa."),
"af15298": dict(
 bluf="Adal Imam Ahmad ibn Ibrahim, called Gragn, the left-handed, set out to conquer Christian Ethiopia with Ottoman muskets. From 1529 he held most of the highlands for over a decade, until Ethiopian and Portuguese troops killed him in 1543. Ethiopia survived as a Christian kingdom, both states were exhausted, and the Horn of Africa became a front in the Ottoman-Portuguese contest.",
 now="The war left both Adal and Ethiopia too weak to hold the lands the Oromo moved into from the 1540s, which remade the map of the Horn. It still colours memory between Ethiopians and Somalis, many of whom regard Imam Ahmad as a hero.",
 story="Adal and Ethiopia had competed for generations over the trade routes to the Red Sea and over highland territory. From 1529 Adal Imam Ahmad ibn Ibrahim, nicknamed Gragn, the left-handed, won a series of victories over Ethiopia with Ottoman-supplied muskets. His forces held most of the highlands for over a decade and sacked churches and monasteries. Ethiopian and Portuguese troops killed him in 1543.",
 why="Adal and Ethiopia had long competed over trade routes to the Red Sea and over highland territory. Ottoman firearms gave Adal the edge in the field.",
 then="With help from about 400 Portuguese musketeers, Ethiopian forces killed Imam Ahmad at Wayna Daga in 1543. Both sides were exhausted, and Oromo pastoralists moved into large parts of the highlands and lowlands.",
 fact="The Portuguese musketeers were led by Cristóvão da Gama, son of Vasco da Gama, who was captured and executed in 1542."),
"af15919": dict(
 bluf="Moroccan Sultan Ahmad al-Mansur sent about 4,000 soldiers, many with muskets, across the Sahara to take the gold of the Sahel and fund a state that could stand up to the Ottomans. At Tondibi in March 1591 they routed Songhai's far larger army of cavalry and archers and took Gao and Timbuktu. Songhai, the last of the great Sahel empires, was destroyed; the Niger bend broke into smaller states, and West Africa's trade turned toward the Atlantic coast where Europeans bought.",
 now="No single state has controlled the Niger bend since 1591. The region's trade and power shifted from the desert routes to the Atlantic coast over the following two centuries, and Timbuktu's decline became the symbol of the end of West Africa's imperial age.",
 story="A disputed succession had weakened Songhai. In 1590 Moroccan Sultan Ahmad al-Mansur sent about 4,000 soldiers, many armed with muskets, across the Sahara under the commander Judar Pasha. At Tondibi in March 1591 they routed Songhai's much larger army of cavalry and archers and took Gao and Timbuktu.",
 why="Al-Mansur wanted the gold of the Sahel to fund his state and compete with the Ottomans. A disputed succession had weakened Songhai.",
 then="The Moroccan commanders in Timbuktu soon became independent of Marrakesh, and the region fragmented. Gold reached Morocco in a fraction of the amounts al-Mansur hoped. Leading Timbuktu men of learning, among them the jurist Ahmad Baba, were deported to Morocco.",
 fact="Tradition holds that Songhai drove a herd of cattle at the Moroccan line and the gunfire turned the animals back into Songhai's own ranks."),
"af165210": dict(
 bluf="The Dutch East India Company wanted a station where its ships to Asia could take on fresh food and water. Commander Jan van Riebeeck built a fort and gardens at Table Bay in 1652; from 1657 the Company released employees as free farmers who pushed into Khoikhoi grazing land, and it imported enslaved people from Asia and Africa. A supply post became a settler colony, the seed of white settlement in South Africa and of the racial order that ended in apartheid.",
 now="The colony's land seizures and slave labour set the pattern of South African society: Dutch-speaking farmers became the Afrikaners, the Khoikhoi lost their land, and the politics of race and land ownership ran from 1652 through apartheid to the present.",
 story="In 1652 Dutch East India Company commander Jan van Riebeeck landed at Table Bay to build a fort and gardens to supply Dutch ships sailing to Asia. From 1657 the Company released some employees as free farmers, who pushed into Khoikhoi grazing lands. It imported enslaved people from Madagascar, Mozambique, India and the East Indies.",
 why="The Dutch East India Company needed fresh food and water for ships on the long voyage to Asia. Free farmers produced it more cheaply than Company gardens.",
 then="Wars and a smallpox epidemic in 1713 destroyed Khoikhoi society. Dutch-speaking farmers spread inland and became the Afrikaners. Britain took the Cape in 1795 and for good in 1806, and the Boers trekked inland from the 1830s.",
 fact="Van Riebeeck planted a hedge of bitter almond to mark the colony's boundary against the Khoikhoi; parts of it survive in Kirstenbosch botanical garden."),
"af169811": dict(
 bluf="Omani Imam Saif bin Sultan set out to drive Portugal from the Swahili coast, as Oman had driven it from Muscat in 1650, and to build an Indian Ocean empire of his own. His fleet besieged Fort Jesus at Mombasa from March 1696 and stormed it in December 1698 after 33 months, when disease and hunger had killed most of the defenders. Portuguese power north of Mozambique ended, the coast was tied to Arabia for two centuries, and Zanzibar's rise as the centre of the clove and slave trades followed.",
 now="Oman's victory replaced Portuguese with Arab control of the Swahili coast and tied East Africa to the Gulf for two centuries, through Zanzibar's clove plantations and the slave caravans into the interior.",
 story="Oman had expelled the Portuguese from Muscat in 1650 and was building its own Indian Ocean empire. In March 1696 a fleet sent by Omani Imam Saif bin Sultan laid siege to Fort Jesus, Portugal's main fortress at Mombasa. Disease and hunger killed most of the defenders. In December 1698 the Omanis stormed the fort after a siege of 33 months.",
 why="Oman wanted the trade of the coast and the Portuguese gone from it. Portuguese relief from Goa arrived too small and too late.",
 then="Oman controlled the coast and its trade for 150 years. Its sultan moved his capital to Zanzibar in 1840.",
 fact="Fort Jesus is a World Heritage site; Omani architecture and vocabulary shaped Swahili culture."),
"af170112": dict(
 bluf="Kumasi ruler Osei Tutu and his priest Okomfo Anokye bound a group of Akan chiefdoms into a confederation to throw off the rule of Denkyira. The alliance crushed Denkyira at Feyiase in 1701, and the Golden Stool, which held the soul of the new nation, gave it a loyalty above local allegiance. Asante became the main power of the Gold Coast, sold gold and captives to Europeans at the coast for guns, and was Britain's hardest opponent there until 1901.",
 now="Asante's union of chiefdoms under one stool held through a series of wars with Britain from 1824 to 1900 and survived annexation. The Asantehene remains the strongest traditional authority in Ghana.",
 story="Denkyira ruled the Akan states of the forest and took tribute from them. Kumasi ruler Osei Tutu and his priest Okomfo Anokye bound a group of Akan chiefdoms into a confederation. By tradition Anokye summoned the Golden Stool from the sky; it held the soul of the nation, and every chief swore loyalty to it, a sacred bond above local allegiances. In 1701 the alliance crushed Denkyira at the battle of Feyiase.",
 why="The Akan states wanted Denkyira's rule and tribute gone. The Golden Stool gave the union a sacred bond above local allegiances.",
 then="Asante expanded and sold gold and captives to Europeans on the coast for guns and goods. After Britain abolished the slave trade in 1807 the two fought a series of wars. Britain annexed Asante in 1901 after the War of the Golden Stool.",
 fact="The Golden Stool, said to have descended from the sky, holds the soul of the Asante nation. No one sits on it, even the king."),
"af179513": dict(
 bluf="Britain seized the Cape in 1795 to keep the station on the sea route to India out of French hands after France overran the Netherlands. A fleet under Admiral George Elphinstone landed troops and took the colony after a short campaign at Muizenberg; Britain handed it back in 1803, retook it in 1806 and kept it. Dutch-speaking settlers came under British rule, and British policy, including the abolition of slavery in 1834, drove thousands of them inland in the Great Trek.",
 now="British rule at the Cape began the conflict between Britain and the Afrikaners, through the Great Trek and the Boer Wars, that shaped Afrikaner nationalism and, in time, apartheid.",
 story="France conquered the Netherlands in 1795. A British fleet under Admiral George Elphinstone landed troops near Cape Town and took the Dutch colony after a short campaign at Muizenberg. Britain handed it back under the Peace of Amiens in 1803, retook it in 1806 and kept it.",
 why="Britain, at war with revolutionary France, wanted the route to India protected. The Cape in French hands would have threatened it.",
 then="British rule, and its abolition of slavery in 1834, angered Dutch-speaking settlers, thousands of whom left the colony in the Great Trek from 1835.",
 fact="The British carried a letter from the exiled Dutch stadtholder William V ordering the Cape to admit them; the Dutch governor refused to act on it."),
"af180414": dict(
 bluf="Fulani cleric Usman dan Fodio launched a jihad in 1804 against the Hausa kings, whom he accused of corruption, heavy taxes and impure Islam. By 1809 his followers had conquered the Hausa city-states and built the Sokoto Caliphate, one of the largest states in 19th-century Africa. Fulani-led Islamic rule united the Hausa lands, survived British conquest in 1903 and still shapes northern Nigeria's politics and religious identity.",
 now="The Sultan of Sokoto remains Nigeria's most senior Muslim leader, and Nigeria's north-south divide partly reflects the caliphate's legacy. Boko Haram claims to revive its jihad, though Sokoto's establishment rejects it.",
 story="The Hausa city-states of today's northern Nigeria were ruled by kings whom reformers accused of mixing Islam with older practice and whose taxes fell on Fulani herders. From 1804 Fulani cleric Usman dan Fodio led a jihad against them. By 1809 his followers had built the Sokoto Caliphate, one of the largest states in 19th-century Africa.",
 why="Protest against corrupt Hausa rulers and heavy taxes came first. The reformers' demand for pure Islamic practice gave it a doctrine. The grievances of Fulani herders gave it an army.",
 trig="The king of Gobir moved against dan Fodio's community in 1804; dan Fodio withdrew to Gudu and declared the jihad.",
 then="The caliphate ruled northern Nigeria until British conquest in 1903. Britain then governed through its emirs, in indirect rule, and preserved the old elite.",
 fact="Dan Fodio's daughter Nana Asma'u, a poet in Arabic, Fulfulde and Hausa, organised a network of women teachers that still carries her name."),
"af181816": dict(
 bluf="Zulu King Shaka kaSenzangakhona set out from about 1816 to make a small chiefdom the dominant power of its region. He drilled its men into regiments armed with short stabbing spears and large shields and conquered or absorbed its neighbours. The Zulu kingdom became the most powerful state in southern Africa, and the wars and migrations around it, the Mfecane, remade the region's peoples just before European settlers pushed inland.",
 now="The kingdom Shaka built fought Britain at Isandlwana in 1879 and survives as a monarchy with political weight in KwaZulu-Natal. The depopulation of the Mfecane years gave Boer trekkers their claim to 'empty' land in the 1830s.",
 story="Shaka kaSenzangakhona took power over the small Zulu chiefdom around 1816. He reorganised its men into disciplined regiments armed with short stabbing spears and large shields. He conquered or absorbed neighbouring chiefdoms and built the Zulu kingdom.",
 why="One reading holds that drought and competition among chiefdoms for land, cattle and water, and for trade at Delagoa Bay, drove the consolidation. The other puts the weight on Shaka's military talent and ambition. Both agree the regiments were his.",
 then="Peoples fled or were absorbed, and new states such as the Ndebele, the Sotho under Moshoeshoe and the Swazi took shape. Boer trekkers later found some areas depopulated and used that to justify claims to 'empty' land.",
 fact="Shaka's half-brothers Dingane and Mhlangana stabbed him to death in 1828; Dingane took the throne."),
"af182217": dict(
 bluf="The American Colonization Society, a coalition of abolitionists who doubted Black Americans would ever be treated as equals and slaveholders who wanted free Black people out of the United States, bought land on the West African coast and sent settlers from 1822. The settlers built a colony at Monrovia, often fighting the peoples already there, and declared Africa's first republic in 1847. Americo-Liberians ruled the indigenous majority for over a century, and that divide fed the coups and civil wars of 1980 to 2003.",
 now="Americo-Liberian rule over the indigenous majority ran from 1847 to the coup of 1980, and the resentment it built fed the civil wars of 1989 to 2003.",
 story="The American Colonization Society, founded in 1816, united some abolitionists with slaveholders who wanted free Black people out of the United States. It bought land on the West African coast and sent settlers from 1822. The newcomers, and Africans freed from slave ships, built a colony at Monrovia, often in conflict with the peoples already living there.",
 why="Abolitionists in the Society believed Black Americans would never be treated equally in the United States. Slaveholders wanted free Black people removed. Most Black Americans rejected the scheme.",
 then="Liberia became Africa's first republic in 1847, ruled by Americo-Liberians. It and Ethiopia were the only African states to keep their independence through the partition.",
 fact="Liberia's capital, Monrovia, is named after US President James Monroe."),
"af183519": dict(
 bluf="Thousands of Boers, Dutch-speaking farmers of the Cape, left British rule from 1835 to escape its abolition of slavery, its English-language administration and its frontier policy. They crossed into the interior in ox-wagons, fought the Ndebele and the Zulu, and founded republics in Natal, the Orange Free State and the Transvaal. The Trek became the founding story of Afrikaner nationalism, and the republics it created were the ground of the Boer War and the ideology of apartheid.",
 now="The Trek created the Boer republics Britain conquered in 1899-1902 and the founding story Afrikaner nationalism used from the 1930s to justify apartheid; the centenary celebrations of 1938 launched the movement that won power in 1948.",
 story="From 1835 thousands of Boers loaded their families into ox-wagons and moved north and east beyond British rule. They fought the Ndebele and, at Blood River in December 1838, the Zulu. They founded republics in Natal, the Orange Free State and the Transvaal.",
 why="Anger at British rule came first: the abolition of slavery in 1834 with what Boers saw as inadequate compensation. Anglicisation of courts and schools and British frontier policy toward the Xhosa added to it.",
 then="Britain annexed Natal in 1843. It recognised the Transvaal and the Orange Free State in the 1850s and conquered them in the Boer War of 1899-1902.",
 fact="Zulu King Dingane killed trek leader Piet Retief and his party in February 1838 after signing a land treaty with them. The Boers' vow before Blood River gave 16 December a public holiday that was renamed the Day of Reconciliation in 1995."),
"af184020": dict(
 bluf="Omani Sultan Said bin Sultan moved his court from Muscat to Zanzibar in 1840 to sit at the centre of the trade that was making the island richer than Oman. He planted cloves across Zanzibar and Pemba, worked by enslaved Africans, and organised the caravan trade in ivory and captives that reached deep into central Africa. Zanzibar became the hub of an East African commercial empire, and its caravan routes were the ones European explorers and colonisers later followed inland.",
 now="The clove plantations and slave caravans built an Arab landowning class over an African labouring majority, a divide that produced the revolution of 1964. The caravan routes opened the interior to European explorers and to the partition that followed from 1884.",
 story="Omani Sultan Said bin Sultan moved his court from Muscat to Zanzibar in 1840. He planted cloves across the islands, worked by enslaved Africans. He made Zanzibar the hub of a caravan trade in ivory and captives reaching deep into central Africa.",
 why="Zanzibar's clove plantations and the ivory and slave caravans from the mainland made it far richer than Muscat. The sultan went where the money was.",
 then="Caravans reached as far as Congo; their routes later guided European explorers and missionaries. After Said's death in 1856 Britain split the empire in 1861 into separate sultanates of Oman and Zanzibar.",
 fact="Zanzibar became the world's leading clove producer. Freddie Mercury was born there in 1946."),
"af-livingstone": dict(
 bluf="The New York Herald sent journalist Henry Morton Stanley to find Scottish missionary and explorer David Livingstone, out of contact in the interior for years, for the sake of the story. Stanley found him at Ujiji on Lake Tanganyika in November 1871, and the report was a sensation on two continents. The meeting made Africa's interior a public obsession and joined exploration, the anti-slavery cause and mass newspapers into the opinion that later backed colonial expansion.",
 now="Livingstone's accounts of the slave caravans, carried to a mass readership by Stanley, built the public pressure that forced Zanzibar to close its slave market in 1873 and gave British intervention in East Africa its justification for the rest of the century.",
 story="Scottish missionary and explorer David Livingstone, campaigning against the East African slave trade, had been out of contact in the interior for years. The New York Herald sent journalist Henry Morton Stanley to find him. Stanley reached him at Ujiji on Lake Tanganyika in November 1871. Livingstone refused to leave and died in the interior in 1873.",
 why="The story sold newspapers. Livingstone's fame as an anti-slavery hero made his fate a public question.",
 then="Livingstone's death in 1873 raised British anti-slavery pressure on Zanzibar. Stanley returned to Africa in 1874 to cross the continent.",
 fact="'Dr Livingstone, I presume?' appears in Stanley's book; the page of his diary for that day was torn out, and Livingstone's own account omits the line."),
"af187322": dict(
 bluf="Britain set out to end the seaborne slave trade of East Africa and threatened a naval blockade to make Zanzibar comply. Zanzibar Sultan Barghash signed a treaty in June 1873 closing the island's slave market, the largest in East Africa, and banning the trade by sea; the trade went on by land and by smuggling. Anti-slavery became the standing justification for British intervention in East Africa and drew the sultanate under British control over the next twenty years.",
 now="The treaty made Britain the enforcer on the coast, and enforcement became control: the protectorate of 1890, the bombardment of 1896 and abolition in 1897 followed from it.",
 story="Zanzibar's market was the largest in East Africa, and the clove plantations and caravan trade depended on it. British envoy Sir Bartle Frere arrived in early 1873 to demand a treaty and was refused. Under the threat of a naval blockade, Zanzibar Sultan Barghash signed a treaty in June 1873 closing the market and banning the seaborne slave trade. The trade continued by land and by smuggling.",
 why="Britain's campaign against the slave trade had public support at home, strengthened by Livingstone's reports. Zanzibar's dependence on British naval protection gave the threat force.",
 then="Slavery itself was abolished in Zanzibar only in 1897. Britain's role as enforcer drew the sultanate steadily under British control, and it became a protectorate in 1890.",
 fact="An Anglican cathedral stands on the site of the market; its altar is where the whipping post used to be."),
"af-stanley": dict(
 bluf="Journalist Henry Morton Stanley set out from Zanzibar in 1874 to settle the geography of the great lakes and the Congo River, and to win fame. He led about 350 people across the continent and down the Congo, fighting his way through; fewer than half survived. The river proved a waterway into the heart of central Africa, and Belgian King Leopold II hired Stanley to claim it.",
 now="Stanley's route gave Leopold II the map and the method for the Congo Free State of 1885. The violence of the expedition set the pattern of the colony's rule.",
 story="Starting from Zanzibar in 1874, journalist Henry Morton Stanley led about 350 people across the continent, circled Lake Victoria and Lake Tanganyika by boat and followed the Congo to its mouth, which he reached in 1877. He fought his way through. Fewer than half survived.",
 why="Stanley, sponsored by the New York Herald and the Daily Telegraph, wanted the geography solved and his name made.",
 then="Belgian King Leopold II founded his International African Association in 1876 and hired Stanley in 1878 to build stations on the Congo.",
 fact="Congolese called Stanley 'Bula Matari', breaker of rocks; the name later attached to the colonial state itself."),
"af187923": dict(
 bluf="British High Commissioner Sir Bartle Frere wanted southern Africa in a confederation under British control and the independent Zulu kingdom removed as an obstacle. He sent Zulu King Cetshwayo an ultimatum to disband his army and invaded in January 1879; the Zulu destroyed a British column at Isandlwana on 22 January, and Britain returned with more troops and burned Ulundi in July. The Zulu kingdom was broken into 13 chiefdoms that fought one another, and Zululand was annexed in 1887.",
 now="The destruction of the kingdom in 1879 and the civil wars among the 13 chiefdoms broke Zulu independence. Annexation in 1887 and incorporation into Natal in 1897 put the Zulu under the settler rule that led to apartheid.",
 story="In January 1879 British forces invaded Zululand after Zulu King Cetshwayo refused an ultimatum to disband his army. At Isandlwana on 22 January a Zulu army killed over 1,300 British and allied soldiers. The same night a small garrison held out at Rorke's Drift. Britain sent reinforcements and burned the royal homestead at Ulundi in July.",
 why="Britain wanted southern Africa in a confederation under its control. High Commissioner Sir Bartle Frere saw the Zulu army as the obstacle and acted without London's approval.",
 then="Britain destroyed the Zulu kingdom and split it into 13 chiefdoms that fought one another. Cetshwayo was exiled, restored in 1883 and died in 1884. Zululand was annexed in 1887.",
 fact="Isandlwana was among the heaviest defeats of a colonial army by an African force. Eleven Victoria Crosses were awarded for Rorke's Drift, the most for a single action."),
"af-peters": dict(
 bluf="German adventurer Carl Peters went inland from the coast in late 1884 to collect treaties with chiefs in Usagara that would give his Society for German Colonisation a claim to East Africa. He gathered them in weeks, often by trickery, and in February 1885 Kaiser Wilhelm I granted his company an imperial charter. The treaties became the legal basis of German East Africa and a claim German Chancellor Otto von Bismarck used in the partition.",
 now="The treaties gave Germany the claim on which it partitioned the mainland with Britain in 1886. The colony became Tanganyika under British rule from 1919 and Tanzania in 1964.",
 story="German adventurer Carl Peters travelled inland from the coast with a few companions in November 1884 and collected 'treaties' from chiefs in Usagara in weeks, often by trickery. The chiefs signed away sovereignty over land the Sultan of Zanzibar claimed. In February 1885 Kaiser Wilhelm I granted Peters' company an imperial charter.",
 why="Germany's leaders, under pressure from colonial lobbyists, wanted colonies to match Britain and France. Peters wanted fame and a chartered company of his own.",
 then="Germany and Britain divided the East African mainland in 1886 and left the Sultan of Zanzibar a coastal strip.",
 fact="Germany dismissed Peters in 1897 for his brutality; Africans called him 'Mkono wa Damu', the bloody hand."),
"af188425": dict(
 bluf="German Chancellor Otto von Bismarck convened 14 states to set rules for claiming African territory so that the European race for colonies would end in treaties rather than a European war. The conference recognised Belgian King Leopold II's Congo, declared free trade on the Congo and Niger and made 'effective occupation' the test of a coastal claim; no African was present. It set off a scramble in which European powers seized almost the whole continent within 30 years and drew borders with little regard for the societies inside them.",
 now="Most of Africa's borders date from the partition and cut across ethnic and political communities. In 1964 African states agreed to keep them anyway, fearing endless wars if they were reopened.",
 story="German Chancellor Otto von Bismarck hosted 14 states, including the United States, in Berlin from November 1884 to February 1885. No Africans were present. The powers agreed rules for claiming African coastline, declared free trade and navigation on the Congo and Niger rivers, and recognised Belgian King Leopold II's claim to the Congo basin. The partition itself was carried out over the next 20 years through bilateral treaties and conquest.",
 why="European rivals wanted to avoid war among themselves while racing for colonies. Industrial demand for raw materials and national prestige drove the race; quinine against malaria, machine guns and river steamers made conquest feasible.",
 then="By 1914 European powers controlled about 90 per cent of Africa; only Ethiopia and Liberia stayed independent. Leopold's Congo Free State became notorious for atrocities.",
 myth="The conference carved up Africa on a map. It set rules for claims and recognised the Congo; the borders were drawn over the next 20 years in bilateral treaties and by conquest.",
 fact="Many of Africa's borders are straight lines drawn in European capitals by men who had never been there."),
"af188526": dict(
 bluf="Belgian King Leopold II wanted a colony that Belgium's parliament would not give him and won the powers' recognition of a private state in the Congo basin by promising free trade and an end to the slave trade. His agents forced Congolese to collect rubber and ivory, held families hostage and cut off hands for missed quotas; the population fell by millions. The atrocities provoked the first international human rights campaign, forced Belgium to annex the colony in 1908 and left Congo a state built for extraction.",
 now="The Free State fixed the Congo as a state organised to extract rather than govern, a pattern Belgian rule to 1960 and Mobutu's regime to 1997 repeated. The campaign against it, organised from 1904, was the model for later human rights movements.",
 story="Belgian King Leopold II had posed as a humanitarian and promised to end the slave trade. His agents forced Congolese to collect rubber and ivory, held families hostage and cut off hands as punishment for missed quotas.",
 why="Leopold wanted a colony that Belgium would not give him. He won recognition at the Berlin Conference by promising free trade and an end to the slave trade. The rubber boom of the 1890s made forced labour pay.",
 then="Campaigners E.D. Morel, Roger Casement and George Washington Williams exposed the abuses. The scandal forced Belgium to annex the colony in 1908. Belgian rule kept extracting resources and left very few Congolese with higher education at independence in 1960.",
 fact="King Leopold II ran Congo as his private property for 23 years and never once visited it."),
"af-1886": dict(
 bluf="Britain and Germany set out to settle their overlapping claims on the East African mainland before they collided. In 1886 they drew a line from the coast to Lake Victoria, bending to put Kilimanjaro on the German side, and left Zanzibar Sultan Barghash, who claimed the whole coast, his islands and a 10-mile strip. The line became the Kenya-Tanzania border, and no African ruler inland was consulted.",
 now="The 1886 line is still the Kenya-Tanzania border. The Sultan's coastal strip, leased and then bought by the European powers, became the coast of both colonies and the ground of the Abushiri revolt of 1888.",
 story="German adventurer Carl Peters' treaties of 1884 had given Germany claims that overlapped Britain's and Zanzibar's. In 1886 Britain and Germany drew a line from the coast to Lake Victoria, bending to put Kilimanjaro on the German side. Britain took the zone to the north, which became Kenya; Germany took the south, mainland Tanzania. Zanzibar Sultan Barghash, who claimed the whole coast, was left his islands and a 10-mile coastal strip, and no African ruler inland was consulted.",
 why="Britain and Germany wanted no conflict over overlapping claims. They settled their spheres without consulting Africans.",
 then="Germany leased the Sultan's coastal strip in 1888, which set off the Abushiri revolt. Britain took over the northern coast.",
 myth="Queen Victoria 'gave' Kilimanjaro to her grandson the Kaiser as a birthday present. The bend came from the 1886 negotiations, and Wilhelm became Kaiser two years later.",
 fact="The border runs almost straight from the coast to the lake except for the bend around Kilimanjaro."),
"af-abushiri": dict(
 bluf="The German East Africa Company took over the Sultan's coastal towns in August 1888 and imposed taxes and land registration; coastal traders, Arab and African leaders and inland allies under Pangani trader Abushiri ibn Salim al-Harthi rose to throw it out. They drove the Germans from almost every town, and Germany sent commander Hermann von Wissmann with an army of African mercenaries to crush them; Abushiri was hanged in December 1889. Company rule had failed, and the German state took direct control of the colony in 1891.",
 now="The revolt ended company rule in German East Africa in 1891 and put the colony under a military administration. Abushiri is honoured in Tanzania as an early resistance leader, and the revolt is an early case of coastal and inland Africans uniting against colonial rule.",
 story="In August 1888 the German East Africa Company took over the coastal towns under its lease from the Sultan and imposed taxes and land registration. Coastal traders, Arab and African leaders and inland allies, led by Pangani trader Abushiri ibn Salim al-Harthi, drove the Germans out of almost every town. Germany sent commander Hermann von Wissmann with an army of African mercenaries. Abushiri was captured and hanged in December 1889.",
 why="The company's arrogance threatened coastal elites' trade, land and religion. Its raising of the German flag over the Sultan's towns was taken as conquest.",
 then="The German state took direct control of German East Africa in 1891 and ended company rule.",
 fact="Wissmann recruited his army in Egypt from Sudanese veterans and added Zulu from Mozambique; it became the colony's Schutztruppe."),
"af-ibeac": dict(
 bluf="Britain wanted influence from Mombasa to Uganda without the cost of direct rule and let a company carry the risk. Shipping magnate William Mackinnon's Imperial British East Africa Company received a royal charter in 1888 to trade and govern the British sphere; it made no profit and was near bankruptcy within five years. The British government took over Uganda in 1894 and Kenya in 1895 directly, and the colonial state replaced the company.",
 now="Company failure made the British state the direct ruler of Uganda from 1894 and Kenya from 1895, and the railway it then built to justify the cost shaped both colonies.",
 story="Scottish shipping magnate William Mackinnon's Imperial British East Africa Company received a royal charter in 1888 to trade and govern the British sphere from Mombasa to Uganda. It sent Captain Frederick Lugard to Buganda in 1890. It made no profit.",
 why="Britain wanted influence in East Africa without the cost of direct rule. A company could carry the risk and the expense.",
 then="The company went nearly bankrupt. Britain took over Uganda in 1894 and Kenya in 1895 directly.",
 fact="Mackinnon had made his fortune with the British India Steam Navigation Company, whose mail steamers had linked Aden and Zanzibar since 1872."),
"af-heligoland": dict(
 bluf="Britain wanted the sources of the Nile in Uganda and a protectorate over Zanzibar; Germany wanted the North Sea island of Heligoland to guard its naval coast. In 1890 they traded: Germany dropped its claims to Uganda and Witu, and Britain got Zanzibar, gave up Heligoland and conceded the Caprivi Strip. Africa's borders were set by a bargain over unrelated interests, and Britain's hold on Zanzibar led to the bombardment of 1896.",
 now="The treaty fixed the sphere Britain turned into Uganda and Kenya and the protectorate it used to govern Zanzibar until 1963. Heligoland became the fortress guarding the Kiel Canal and the German fleet.",
 story="British Prime Minister Lord Salisbury and German Chancellor Leo von Caprivi concluded the treaty on 1 July 1890. Britain got Zanzibar as a protectorate, and Germany dropped claims in Uganda and Witu. Germany got the island of Heligoland and the Caprivi Strip in Namibia.",
 why="Britain wanted control of the Nile's sources and Zanzibar. Germany wanted Heligoland to protect its naval coast and the Kiel Canal then under construction.",
 then="Britain's grip on Zanzibar led to the 1896 bombardment. Germany turned Heligoland into a naval fortress.",
 fact="The Caprivi Strip's odd shape on Namibia's map comes from this treaty; Germany wanted a corridor to the Zambezi."),
"af-lugalo": dict(
 bluf="German commander Emil von Zelewski marched into the southern highlands to break Hehe control of the caravan routes after Hehe Chief Mkwawa refused to submit. On 17 August 1891 about 3,000 Hehe warriors ambushed his column of about 320 askari plus officers and porters at Lugalo and killed him. It was the heaviest early defeat of colonial forces in German East Africa, and it forced Germany into a seven-year war that made the colony a military one.",
 now="The defeat forced Germany to fight a seven-year war against the Hehe and to garrison the interior, which made German East Africa a military colony; Mkwawa's resistance became a founding story of Tanzanian nationalism.",
 story="The Hehe under Chief Mkwawa were the strongest power in the southern highlands and controlled the caravan routes. On 17 August 1891 about 3,000 Hehe warriors ambushed German commander Emil von Zelewski's force of about 320 askari plus officers and porters at Lugalo. Zelewski was killed.",
 why="Germany wanted Hehe control of the trade routes broken. Mkwawa refused to submit.",
 then="Germans stormed Mkwawa's fortress at Kalenga in 1894. He fought a guerrilla war until 1898.",
 fact="The Hehe carried off the column's rifles and guns. Lugalo is celebrated in Tanzania as a symbol of resistance."),
"af-uganda": dict(
 bluf="The British government declared a protectorate over Uganda in 1894 to keep the source of the Nile, and with it Egypt's water, out of another power's hands after the Imperial British East Africa Company could no longer afford to hold it. Buganda's kingdom became the core of a British colony. Strategic worry about water drove the colonisation of a whole region and tied Uganda's fate to Egypt's for the rest of the century.",
 now="Britain's Nile strategy governed East Africa to 1956: Uganda, Kenya and Sudan were held to secure Egypt's water. The Nile still shapes the region's politics, including the dispute over Ethiopia's Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam.",
 story="The Imperial British East Africa Company could not afford to hold Uganda and announced its withdrawal in 1892. Missionary lobbies and Nile strategists in Britain campaigned to stay. The British government declared a protectorate in 1894, with Buganda's king and chiefs as its partners.",
 why="Control of the source of the Nile protected Britain's position in Egypt. Missionary and anti-slavery opinion wanted Buganda's Protestants protected.",
 then="Britain built a railway from Mombasa to Lake Victoria to hold it. It ruled largely through the Buganda kingdom's chiefs, whose position the Uganda Agreement of 1900 confirmed.",
 fact="Uganda takes its name from Buganda, the kingdom at its centre, as Swahili speakers rendered it."),
"af-uganda-b": dict(
 bluf="Britain took over the Imperial British East Africa Company's territory between the coast and Uganda in July 1895 to secure the route to the Nile. The East Africa Protectorate, run from Mombasa, moved its capital to Nairobi in 1905 and became Kenya Colony in 1920. A railway and white settlement in the highlands shaped the colony, and the land taken for settlers became the central grievance of Kenyan nationalism.",
 now="The land alienated for settlers from 1902 was the grievance behind the Mau Mau uprising of 1952-60 and the land politics of independent Kenya.",
 story="In July 1895 Britain took over the company's territory between the coast and Uganda as the East Africa Protectorate, with its capital at Mombasa. The capital moved to Nairobi in 1905. The territory became Kenya Colony in 1920.",
 why="Britain wanted the route between the coast and Uganda secured, and with it the headwaters of the Nile.",
 then="The railway and white settlers in the highlands followed. Africans were pushed into reserves.",
 fact="Kenya is named after its mountain, which the Kikuyu call Kirinyaga."),
"af-railway": dict(
 bluf="Britain built the Uganda Railway from Mombasa to Lake Victoria to hold Uganda and the Nile's headwaters, end the inland slave caravans and make the colony pay. Begun in 1896 with over 30,000 labourers from British India, several thousand of whom died, it reached the lake in 1901. It fixed Kenya's geography: Nairobi, white settlement in the highlands and the Indian community all grew from it.",
 now="The line decided where Kenya's capital, settler farms and towns would be. Nairobi exists because of the railway, Kenya's Indian community descends partly from its workers, and the settler economy it was built to pay for created the land grievance of 1952.",
 story="Begun in 1896 at Mombasa, the railway reached Lake Victoria at Kisumu in 1901. Over 30,000 labourers came from British India; several thousand died. In 1898 two lions at Tsavo killed at least 28 workers. Nairobi grew from a railway camp.",
 why="Britain wanted Uganda and the Nile secured. It wanted the inland slave caravans ended and the colony paying for itself.",
 then="White settlers took fertile highlands along the line. Indian traders and workers settled in towns along its route.",
 fact="Critics in Parliament mocked it as 'the Lunatic Line' because it ran from nowhere to nowhere; it cost over £5 million."),
"af189627": dict(
 bluf="Italy claimed a protectorate over Ethiopia under the Treaty of Wuchale and invaded to enforce it; Ethiopian Emperor Menelik II raised about 100,000 men armed with modern rifles to stop it. At Adwa on 1 March 1896 his army destroyed the Italian force, killing about 7,000, and Italy recognised Ethiopia's independence that October. Ethiopia stayed free while almost all of Africa was colonised, and Adwa became the proof that an African state could beat a European army.",
 now="Adwa kept Ethiopia independent until 1936 and made it the reference point of pan-Africanism. Ethiopia's colours appear on many African flags, and Addis Ababa hosts the African Union.",
 story="Italy claimed a protectorate under a clause of the 1889 Treaty of Wuchale whose Amharic and Italian versions differed. On 1 March 1896 Ethiopian Emperor Menelik II's army of around 100,000 men, armed with modern rifles, destroyed an Italian invasion force under General Oreste Baratieri at Adwa. Empress Taytu led her own contingent. About 7,000 Italian soldiers were killed. Italy recognised Ethiopia's independence that October.",
 why="Italy wanted Ethiopia as the hinterland of its colony of Eritrea. Menelik had united Ethiopia's regional lords and built a large army with modern rifles, many bought from France and Russia.",
 then="Ethiopia and Liberia were the only African states to keep their independence through the partition. Italy invaded again under Mussolini in 1935.",
 fact="Ethiopia's green, yellow and red were later adopted by many newly independent African states as pan-African colours."),
"af189628": dict(
 bluf="Britain claimed the right to approve Zanzibar's sultan, and when Khalid bin Barghash seized the palace on Sultan Hamad's death in August 1896 it chose to make an example. On 27 August British warships shelled the palace for about 40 minutes until Khalid's flag came down; about 500 of his defenders were killed or wounded. Britain installed a sultan who did as it asked, and the sultanate's independent authority ended.",
 now="The bombardment installed a sultan who did as Britain asked, ended Zanzibar's independence and forced the abolition of slavery in 1897; British control of the coast and the Swahili interior followed.",
 story="Zanzibar Sultan Hamad died on 25 August 1896. His cousin Khalid bin Barghash seized the palace without British approval. On 27 August British warships shelled the palace for about 40 minutes until Khalid's flag came down. About 500 of his defenders were killed or wounded.",
 why="Khalid seized the palace without British approval. Britain, which claimed the right to approve the sultan under the 1890 protectorate, chose to make an example.",
 then="Britain installed Hamoud bin Mohammed as sultan. He abolished slavery in 1897. Zanzibar remained a British protectorate until 1963.",
 fact="At about forty minutes, it is the shortest recorded war. Khalid fled to the German consulate and was carried out to a German warship."),
"af-zanzslavery": dict(
 bluf="Britain pressed Zanzibar to abolish slavery to justify the protectorate as an anti-slavery mission. In April 1897 Zanzibar Sultan Hamoud bin Mohammed abolished the legal status of slavery on Zanzibar and Pemba in a cautious decree: enslaved people had to apply to a court, owners were compensated and concubines were excluded until 1909. Few applied at first, since freedom brought no land or income, and the inequality between Arab landowners and African labourers that remained fed the revolution of 1964.",
 now="Zanzibar's 1964 revolution, which overthrew the Arab-dominated government, was rooted partly in the inequalities left by slavery.",
 story="In April 1897 Zanzibar Sultan Hamoud bin Mohammed, under British pressure, abolished the legal status of slavery on Zanzibar and Pemba. Enslaved people had to apply to a court for freedom, owners were compensated and concubines were excluded until 1909. Relatively few applied at first, because freedom brought no land or income. Many stayed on the clove plantations as low-paid workers or squatters.",
 why="British pressure after the 1896 bombardment came first. Britain needed to justify its protectorate as an anti-slavery mission.",
 then="Slavery on the mainland lingered for years. Kenya's coast ended it only in 1907, and German East Africa never fully abolished it.",
 fact="The decree came 24 years after the market closed and 90 years after Britain banned its own slave trade."),
"af-mkwawa": dict(
 bluf="German forces had hunted Hehe Chief Mkwawa for four years after his fortress at Kalenga fell in 1894. Surrounded at Mlambalasi on 19 July 1898, he took his own life rather than surrender, and German soldiers cut off his head as a trophy. The Hehe resistance, one of the longest against German rule, ended, and the return of his skull from Germany in 1954 became an early case of colonial restitution.",
 now="Mkwawa is a national hero in Tanzania, and the return of his skull is an early landmark in debates over restitution of colonial-era remains and objects.",
 story="Hehe Chief Mkwawa had fought a guerrilla war since his fortress at Kalenga fell in 1894. Surrounded at Mlambalasi near Iringa, he took his own life on 19 July 1898. German soldiers cut off his head as a trophy and sent the skull to Germany.",
 why="Germany wanted the Hehe broken and the southern caravan routes secured. Mkwawa refused to submit.",
 then="His skull was held in Bremen. It was returned in 1954 and is in a memorial museum at Kalenga.",
 fact="Article 246 of the Treaty of Versailles of 1919 ordered Germany to hand over 'the skull of the Sultan Mkwawa'. It took 35 years."),
"af-omdurman": dict(
 bluf="British commander Herbert Kitchener led an Anglo-Egyptian army up the Nile to destroy the Mahdist state, secure the Nile valley and avenge General Charles Gordon's death at Khartoum in 1885. Outside Omdurman on 2 September 1898 his Maxim guns and artillery killed about 10,000 of some 50,000 Mahdist troops for fewer than 50 British dead. The Mahdist state was destroyed, and Britain and Egypt ruled Sudan jointly until 1956.",
 now="Anglo-Egyptian rule from 1899 governed Sudan's north and south as separate worlds, which deepened the division that produced the civil wars of 1955 to 2005 and South Sudan's secession in 2011.",
 story="Mahdist Sudan under Khalifa Abdallahi had ruled since the fall of Khartoum in 1885. British commander Herbert Kitchener's Anglo-Egyptian army, with Maxim guns and artillery, faced about 50,000 Mahdist troops outside Omdurman on 2 September 1898. About 10,000 Sudanese were killed; fewer than 50 British troops died.",
 why="Britain wanted the Nile valley secured against France. Gordon's death in 1885 gave the campaign its public cause.",
 then="Britain and Egypt ruled Sudan jointly as the Anglo-Egyptian Sudan until 1956. Kitchener marched on to Fashoda to face the French.",
 fact="Winston Churchill rode in the charge of the 21st Lancers at Omdurman and wrote it up in 'The River War'."),
"af-omdurman-b": dict(
 bluf="France sent Major Jean-Baptiste Marchand across Africa to plant its flag on the Upper Nile and claim a belt of territory from west to east; Britain wanted the whole Nile from Cairo to Uganda. Marchand reached Fashoda in July 1898 and British commander Herbert Kitchener arrived with a far larger force after Omdurman; after weeks of tension France, with the weaker navy, backed down. The Nile went to Britain, and the two rivals settled their colonial disputes in the Entente Cordiale of 1904.",
 now="Fashoda settled that France would leave the Nile to Britain, which cleared the way for the Entente Cordiale of 1904 and the alliance that fought Germany ten years later.",
 story="French Major Jean-Baptiste Marchand's small expedition had crossed Africa over two years to plant the flag at Fashoda on the White Nile in July 1898. British commander Herbert Kitchener arrived in September with a much larger force after Omdurman. After weeks of tension France backed down.",
 why="France wanted a belt of territory across Africa from west to east. Britain wanted the whole Nile. France's navy could not fight Britain's.",
 then="France ordered Marchand to withdraw in November 1898. The two settled their colonial disputes in the Entente Cordiale of 1904.",
 fact="'Fashoda syndrome' is still used for French wariness of British influence in Africa."),
"af189929": dict(
 bluf="Britain went to war with the Transvaal and the Orange Free State to control the Witwatersrand gold fields and bring southern Africa under its rule. It won in 1902 after burning farms and holding about 100,000 civilians in camps where about 26,000 Boer civilians, most of them children, and at least 14,000 Black Africans died. The Union of South Africa of 1910 reconciled British and Boer whites at the expense of Black South Africans.",
 now="The camps and farm-burning of 1900-02 were an early case of 20th-century warfare against civilians, and the bitterness they left fed the Afrikaner nationalism that won power in 1948 and built apartheid.",
 story="Gold had been found on the Witwatersrand in 1886 and Britain wanted it. War began in October 1899 after Transvaal President Paul Kruger's ultimatum. Boer guerrillas fought on after the republics' capitals fell in 1900; Britain burned farms and held about 100,000 civilians in camps where tens of thousands died.",
 why="Control of the Witwatersrand gold fields came first. British demands for rights for foreign miners in the Transvaal gave a cause. Imperial ambition to unite southern Africa under British rule set the goal.",
 trig="Transvaal President Paul Kruger's ultimatum of 9 October 1899 demanding that British troops withdraw from the Transvaal border.",
 then="Britain won at Vereeniging in May 1902. In 1910 it created the Union of South Africa, a white-ruled dominion that excluded Black Africans from power.",
 fact="Welsh campaigner Emily Hobhouse's report on the camps in 1901 forced a government inquiry and a change of camp administration."),
"d-goldenstool": dict(
 bluf="Gold Coast Governor Frederick Hodgson demanded in March 1900 to sit on the Golden Stool, the seat of Asante's soul, to complete the control Britain had taken by exiling the Asantehene in 1896. Queen Mother Yaa Asantewaa of Ejisu led Asante fighters in a siege of the British fort at Kumasi for months before a relief force broke the revolt. Britain annexed Asante to the Gold Coast in 1901, the Stool stayed out of its hands, and the sovereignty it stood for outlived the conquest.",
 now="The Stool's survival let Asante keep its identity under colonial rule. Britain restored the Asantehene in 1926 and the Asante Confederacy in 1935, and the Asantehene remains the strongest traditional authority in Ghana.",
 story="Britain had exiled Asantehene Prempeh I in 1896. In March 1900 Gold Coast Governor Frederick Hodgson demanded the Golden Stool, the symbol of the Asante nation. Queen Mother Yaa Asantewaa of Ejisu led Asante fighters, who besieged the British fort at Kumasi for months. A relief force broke the revolt.",
 why="Britain wanted complete control after exiling the king. The demand for the Stool struck at the one bond above all Asante chiefs.",
 then="Asante was annexed to the Gold Coast colony in 1901. Yaa Asantewaa was exiled to the Seychelles, where she died in 1921. The Stool stayed hidden.",
 fact="Yaa Asantewaa is a national heroine in Ghana and a symbol of women's leadership in anti-colonial resistance. The Stool was kept from the British and is still never sat on, even by the king."),
"af190430": dict(
 bluf="German settlers in South West Africa had taken Herero and Nama land and cattle, and the Herero rose in January 1904, the Nama after them. German General Lothar von Trotha ordered the Herero exterminated, drove survivors into the Omaheke desert and held the rest in concentration camps; up to 80 per cent of the Herero and about half of the Nama died. It is widely recognised as the first genocide of the 20th century, and its extermination order, camps and racial science reappeared in the atrocities that followed.",
 now="Germany acknowledged it as genocide in 2021 and offered development aid; many Herero and Nama groups rejected the deal as inadequate, and the land question it left is still open in Namibia.",
 story="German settlers had seized Herero and Nama land and cattle. The Herero under Chief Samuel Maharero rose in January 1904, and the Nama under Hendrik Witbooi followed in October. German General Lothar von Trotha issued an extermination order in October 1904 after the battle of Waterberg. Survivors were driven into the desert or concentration camps.",
 why="German settlers seized Herero and Nama land and cattle. Abuse and debt schemes drove the Herero to revolt, and the Nama followed.",
 then="Up to 80 per cent of the Herero and about half of the Nama died. Survivors were held in camps, and their land and cattle were confiscated. Berlin rescinded the order in December 1904, after most of the killing.",
 fact="Germany formally acknowledged it as genocide in 2021. Skulls of the dead sent to Germany for racial research were returned in 2011 and 2018."),
}
